{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 /workspace/tools.py read queryformer-paper-v1 --find '4.2.1 Query-Generation Pipeline' --lines 65\"","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"queryformer-paper-v1\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.16548v1\",\n  \"version\": \"arxiv:2609.16548v1\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"archived exact-version primary text; caching does not establish a local mechanism result\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"f8e114e22e2e8016610748784b98f2ffb59e5611f92ef03689e11f7aed4de3a6\",\n  \"total_lines\": 591,\n  \"start_line\": 24,\n  \"next_line\": 89,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"24: 4.1 Overview\\n25: 4.2 Query-Driven Architecture\\n26: 4.2.1 Query-Generation Pipeline\\n27: 4.2.2 Embedding Matrix\\n28: 4.2.3 Co-Transformer\\n29: 4.2.4 Retrieval\\n30: 4.2.5 SeqQueryCrossAttn\\n31: 4.3 Supporting Components\\n32: 4.3.1 NS Token Construction\\n33: 4.3.2 Dense Feature Fusion\\n34: 4.3.3 Sequence Token Embedding\\n35: 4.3.4 Blocks\\n36: 4.4 Training Recipe\\n37: 4.4.1 Multi-Loss Supervision\\n38: 4.4.2 Optimization\\n39: 5 Experiments\\n40: 5.1 Experimental Setup\\n41: 5.2 Ablation Study\\n42: 5.3 Competition Results\\n43: 5.4 Scaling Analysis\\n44: 5.4.1 Architectural Scaling Efficiency\\n45: 6 Conclusion\\n46: References\\n47: License: CC BY 4.0\\n48: arXiv:2609.16548v1 [cs.AI] 15 Sep 2026\\n49: QueryFormer: Winning Solution for KDD Cup 2026 Tencent UniRec Challenge\\n50: Conference: KDD Cup 2026 Tencent UniRec Challenge Workshop; August 12, 2026; Jeju, Korea\\n51: Yuanzhe Zhou\\n52: Affiliation: Wuhan University\\n53: email:\\n54: and\\n55: Zhaoyang Zeng\\n56: Affiliation: Sun Yat-sen University\\n57: email:\\n58: 2026\\n59: Abstract.\\n60: Post-click conversion rate (pCVR) prediction requires jointly modeling feature interactions and sequential user behaviors. The KDD Cup 2026 Tencent UniRec Challenge calls for a unified architecture addressing both. We observe that existing unified architectures often generate query tokens—the central information hub—with projection-based multi-layer perceptrons (MLPs), without explicit token-to-query attention for refining the query side. We propose QueryFormer, centered on a stackable unified field–sequence block that bridges non-sequential multi-field features and behavioral sequences, and provide a latency-aware scaling study over view width HH, model width, depth, data, and compute. The block generates queries through cross-attention and packs sequence queries into shared-parameter attention. QueryFormer secured 1st place in the Industrial Track, achieving an official test area under the ROC curve (AUC) of 0.83254; a modest post-competition scale-up reached 0.832713. Within our grid, HH-scaling improves validation AUC from 0.84540 to 0.84615 and beats HyFormer at comparable budgets. Ablation identifies query generation as the largest contributor. Packed shared-parameter cross-attention keeps H=8H{=}8 inference latency to only 1.89×1.89{\\\\times} that of H=1H{=}1, positioning the bridge as an efficient stackable unified block.\\n61: Architecture diagram showing QueryFormer's query-driven bridge from non-sequential tokens to behavioral sequences through CoTransformerBlock, QuerySeqCrossAttn, and SeqQueryCrossAttn, with H independent tokenization columns.\\n62: Figure 1. QueryFormer architecture. The reusable stackable query-generation bridge takes non-sequential field tokens and behavioral sequence tokens as input, returns generated query probes and mixed representations, and preserves the HH dimension as parallel tokenization columns with shape-(Bs,H,T,dmodel)(B_{s},H,T,d_{\\\\text{model}}) views, where BsB_{s} is batch size and TT is token count, before per-column outputs are combined.Architecture diagram showing QueryFormer's query-driven bridge from non-sequential tokens to behavioral sequences through CoTransformerBlock, QuerySeqCrossAttn, and SeqQueryCrossAttn, with H independent tokenization columns.\\n63: Figure 2. Scaling evidence for the stackable query-generation bridge. (a) Parameter scaling: QueryFormer traces a stronger AUC–dense-parameter frontier than HyFormer (Huang et al., 2026) within the explored budgets, with separate dmodeld_{\\\\text{model}}- and HH-scaling paths. (b) Data efficiency: QueryFormer (Pdense=87.1​MP_{\\\\mathrm{dense}}{=}87.1\\\\mathrm{M}, H=4H{=}4) remains above HyFormer (Pdense=105.8​MP_{\\\\mathrm{dense}}{=}105.8\\\\mathrm{M}) from about 20% to full training data. Both x-axes are logarithmic; PdenseP_{\\\\mathrm{dense}} denotes dense parameter count.Two-panel scaling analysis. (a) Parameter scaling: AUC vs dense parameters for QueryFormer d\\\\_model-scaling, H-scaling, and HyFormer. (b) Data efficiency: AUC vs training samples for HyFormer (P\\\\_dense=105.8M) and QueryFormer (P\\\\_dense=87.1M, H=4). Both subplots use log x-axes.\\n64: 1. Introduction\\n65: The KDD Cup 2026 Tencent UniRec Challenge requires post-click conversion rate (pCVR) models that jointly handle multi-field features and user behavior sequences. Our solution, QueryFormer, won the Industrial Track. The key observation is that existing unified architectures often generate query tokens with projection-based multi-layer perceptrons (MLPs), which provide nonlinear context compression but lack explicit token-to-query attention. Since the field-to-sequence cross-attention channel ⟨N,B⟩\\\\langle N,B\\\\rangle (non-sequential field tokens attending to behavioral sequence tokens) is dominant (Liu et al., 2026), query quality directly determines what sequence evidence is retrieved and how user-item context is aggregated.\\n66: QueryFormer redesigns query processing as a stronger query-generation mechanism. It combines token-level self/cross attention, item-to-sequence retrieval, and cross-attention-based query generation (Figure 1). Its reusable unit is a stackable unified field–sequence block: field and behavior tokens enter a token-in/token-out bridge, generated query probes retrieve sequence evidence, and packed shared-parameter attention keeps multi-view execution efficient. Ablations show that replacing SeqQueryCrossAttn with an MLP causes the largest degradation, and scaling the embedding matrix width HH gives consistent gains. Figure 2 previews the scaling results against HyFormer.\\n67: Our key contributions are:\\n68: (1)\\n69: Query Generation Mechanism: cross-attention-driven query generation adds explicit token-to-query attention beyond projection-based MLP generation.\\n70: (2)\\n71: Unified Block: a token-in/token-out field–sequence bridge composes with token-based recommenders and uses packed shared-parameter attention.\\n72: (3)\\n73: Latency-Aware Scaling Study: H=4H=4 independent tokenization columns form a shape-(Bs,H,T,dmodel)(B_{s},H,T,d_{\\\\text{model}}) embedding matrix, enabling batch-parallel scaling across views, depth, width, data, and compute.\\n74: (4)\\n75: Industrial Validation: 1st place in the KDD Cup 2026 Industrial Track (test AUC 0.83254) with consistent scaling trends across multiple dimensions.\\n76: 2. Related Work\\n77: 2.1. Feature Interaction and Sequence Modeling\\n78: DeepFM (Guo et al., 2017), DCN-V2 (Wang et al., 2021), Fi-GNN (Li et al., 2019), and DLRM (Naumov et al., 2019) model static feature interactions through product, cross-network, graph, or embedding-interaction layers. Sequential recommenders such as DIN (Zhou et al., 2018), DIEN (Zhou et al., 2019), SIM (Pi et al., 2020), BERT4Rec (Sun et al., 2019), SASRec (Kang and McAuley, 2018), and HSTU (Zhai et al., 2024) extract interest from behavior histories. These lines are usually optimized as separate modules; QueryFormer instead lets field tokens and behavior evidence interact through a unified query pipeline.\\n79: 2.2. Token-Based Architectures\\n80: Recent token-based models (Ding et al., 2023; Huang et al., 2026; Zhang et al., 2026; Zhou et al., 2026) represent heterogeneous recommendation features as tokens processed by Transformer-style blocks. EST (Liu et al., 2026) shows that ⟨N,B⟩\\\\langle N,B\\\\rangle cross-attention is the dominant interaction channel, but it does not optimize how the query side is generated. HyFormer and OneTrans reuse MLP-generated queries; LENS, GAP-Net, and HeMix calibrate MLP queries with gates, cascades, or mixed static/dynamic components. QueryFormer takes a stronger step: it generates query probes through cross-attention and repeatedly refines them through a query-centric pipeline (Table 1). In Table 1, Bridge describes how explicitly a method connects non-sequential field tokens with behavioral sequence evidence during query generation.\\n81: Table 1. Unified-block positioning. Tok→\\\\toQ: explicit token-to-query attention; token I/O: token input/output; Packed: shared-parameter packed attention for multi-view queries.\\n82: Method\\n83: Query\\n84: Tok→\\\\toQ\\n85: Bridge\\n86: Views\\n87: Packed\\n88: HyFormer (Huang et al., 2026)\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
