{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'python3 /workspace/tools.py read queryformer-paper-v1 --start-line 175 --lines 65'","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"queryformer-paper-v1\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.16548v1\",\n  \"version\": \"arxiv:2609.16548v1\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"archived exact-version primary text; caching does not establish a local mechanism result\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"f8e114e22e2e8016610748784b98f2ffb59e5611f92ef03689e11f7aed4de3a6\",\n  \"total_lines\": 591,\n  \"start_line\": 175,\n  \"next_line\": 240,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"175: Figure 3. Validation AUC and LogLoss vs. training steps for H∈{1,2,4,8}H\\\\in\\\\{1,2,4,8\\\\} at dmodel=272d_{\\\\text{model}}{=}272.Training curves showing that wider embedding matrices achieve better final AUC and LogLoss.\\n176: 4.2.3. Co-Transformer\\n177: The Co-Transformer contains two layers. Each layer first applies QuerySelfAttn within user and item token sets, then QueryCrossAttn between user and item tokens with gated fusion. This separates within-field refinement from cross-side interaction and lets us ablate the two effects independently.\\n178: 4.2.4. Retrieval\\n179: QuerySeqCrossAttn retrieves behavior evidence by using aggregated item NS tokens as queries over each of the four behavior domains. The retrieved vectors summarize item-relevant historical evidence and are appended to the NS token set.\\n180: 4.2.5. SeqQueryCrossAttn\\n181: SeqQueryCrossAttn generates Nq=2N_{q}=2 query probes per sequence domain. Mean/max-pooled behavior summaries initialize probes, and cross-attention over refined NS tokens makes them context-aware:\\n182: (6)\\n183: 𝐪d,k=CrossAttnd​(𝐪d,k(0),𝐍,𝐍).\\\\mathbf{q}_{d,k}=\\\\mathrm{CrossAttn}_{d}(\\\\mathbf{q}_{d,k}^{(0)},\\\\mathbf{N},\\\\mathbf{N}).\\n184: 4.3. Supporting Components\\n185: 4.3.1. NS Token Construction\\n186: NS tokens are built from user/item integer features, dense feature slices, temporal features, item ID, and four retrieved sequence tokens, yielding 26 NS tokens.\\n187: 4.3.2. Dense Feature Fusion\\n188: User and item dense features are processed by DenseFusionModel, which combines a low-rank DCN-V2 layer (L=1L=1, rank r=8r=8), a SiLU-activated MLP, and Squeeze-and-Excitation (SENet) recalibration (Hu et al., 2018).\\n189: 4.3.3. Sequence Token Embedding\\n190: Each behavior domain embeds side-information independently, projects it to dmodeld_{\\\\text{model}}, and adds a 65-bucket recency embedding.\\n191: 4.3.4. Blocks\\n192: Following HyFormer and OneTrans (Huang et al., 2026; Zhang et al., 2026), K=2K=2 lightweight processing blocks evolve behavior sequences with Swish-Gated Linear Unit (SwiGLU) encoders, attend from queries to sequences, and mix tokens through parameter-free permutation.\\n193: 4.4. Training Recipe\\n194: 4.4.1. Multi-Loss Supervision\\n195: The training objective combines binary cross-entropy (BCE) with an auxiliary mean absolute error (MAE) loss on log-transformed conversion time (λ=0.1\\\\lambda=0.1). Removing it reduces validation AUC from 0.84606 to 0.84590, a smaller effect than any attention ablation.\\n196: 4.4.2. Optimization\\n197: We use MuonPlus for dense parameters, Adagrad for sparse embeddings, CosineAnnealingLR, gradient clipping at ℓ2\\\\ell_{2}-norm 1.0, EMA decay 0.999, bfloat16, and torch.compile.\\n198: 5. Experiments\\n199: 5.1. Experimental Setup\\n200: Input data is stored in Apache Parquet format with a 90/10 Row-Group-level train/validation split across 142 feature columns and four behavioral domains (a, b, c, d). All experiments use a fixed sequence limit Lseq=256L_{\\\\mathrm{seq}}=256: sequences are padded to 256 positions and longer histories are truncated to the most recent positions. Labels include conversion type and log-transformed conversion timestamp.\\n201: We evaluate on the Round 2 Industrial Track dataset using area under the ROC curve (AUC) as the primary metric, coupled with LogLoss (logarithmic loss) and inference latency constraints. In this leaderboard setting, AUC differences around 10−410^{-4} were ranking-relevant; the top two official submissions differ by 0.00037. QueryFormer targets the competition’s innovation criteria with a stackable field–sequence bridge and a latency-aware scaling study over HH, KK, dmodeld_{\\\\text{model}}, training length, data, and compute cost. Evidence is organized as follows: Table 3 compares the optimized recipe with HyFormer, Table 4 isolates the bridge mechanisms, Table 6 measures packed-attention latency, and Table 7/Figure 2 summarize multi-axis scaling.\\n202: Validation AUC is consistently higher than test AUC (best val 0.84631 vs. test 0.83254), reflecting the expected distribution shift between temporally proximate train/val splits and the held-out test period. The relative ordering was preserved for submitted architectural and scaling changes, so we report validation AUC for most experiments due to limited test submission slots. Models are trained on NVIDIA GPUs with multi-GPU distributed data parallel (DDP) via torchrun. The PyTorch implementation uses a custom IterableDataset with pre-allocated NumPy buffers, row-group-balanced DDP sharding, EMA-shadow validation, and sidecar configs for checkpoint recovery. For final submission, training on the full 35M samples (no validation holdout) yields ∼\\\\sim++0.0001 AUC, and ensemble distillation with multiple variants supervising a single student (Tang and Wang, 2018; Ding et al., 2026; Lai et al., 2025; Zhu et al., 2020) yields ∼\\\\sim++0.0007 test AUC at single-model inference cost. Due to the competition timeline, we did not exhaustively sweep all high-capacity configurations; the post-competition result suggests additional gains remain available.\\n203: Table 3. Validation comparison under the optimized training recipe. Dense parameters are in millions; all runs use Lseq=256L_{\\\\mathrm{seq}}{=}256.\\n204: Model\\n205: HH\\n206: Dense\\n207: Val AUC\\n208: LogLoss\\n209: HyFormer (Huang et al., 2026)\\n210: –\\n211: 105.8\\n212: 0.84477\\n213: –\\n214: QueryFormer\\n215: 4\\n216: 87.1\\n217: 0.84606\\n218: 0.210309\\n219: QueryFormer\\n220: 4\\n221: 220.8\\n222: 0.84631\\n223: 0.210241\\n224: 5.2. Ablation Study\\n225: Table 4 reports ablation results; Figure 4 shows per-epoch trends. The degradation order—cross-attention query generation causes the largest drop, followed by cross-attention, sequence attention, and self-attention—confirms that query quality is the primary performance driver. Removing all four mechanisms compounds the loss (Δ=−0.00110\\\\Delta=-0.00110), validating the additive benefit of each component.\\n226: Table 4. Ablation of attention mechanisms (H=4H{=}4, dmodel=272d_{\\\\text{model}}{=}272).\\n227: Configuration\\n228: Best AUC\\n229: Δ\\\\Delta\\n230: Full Model (baseline)\\n231: 0.84606\\n232: —\\n233: - Replace SeqQueryCrossAttn with MLP\\n234: 0.84568\\n235: −-0.00038\\n236: - Remove QueryCrossAttn\\n237: 0.84577\\n238: −-0.00029\\n239: - Remove QuerySeqCrossAttn\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
